            <div xml:id="d802">

                <pb facs="0996" n="872" sameAs="frus:frus1921v01/pg872"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">422.11 Am 8/6: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Ecuador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hartman</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Quito</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1921-11-15T11:00:00-05:14"><hi rendition="#italic">November 15,
                                1921—11 a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received November 16—7:30
                        p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>34. My 33, November 14th, 11 a.m. [<hi rendition="#italic">p.m</hi>]<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d802-f1"/> I had an interview with President yesterday afternoon. He
                    stated that the president of the council of Guayaquil had informed him that the
                    municipality has sufficient money set aside to pay Amsinck claim but that United
                        <pb facs="0997" n="873" xml:id="pg873"/> States drafts cannot be obtained.
                    He said that he believes present members of municipal council are acting in good
                    faith. I suggest that Amsinck and Company arrange to receive payment in sucres
                    at such rate as may be agreed upon and have same deposited to their credit in
                    some bank in Ecuador until such time as United States drafts can be obtained.
                    This will prevent possible diversion of funds to other purposes.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hartman</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
